y very  difficult time  for lots of businesses where people will  be concerned and worried about their  jobs about their livelihoods  about their families and it is right and  proper that as a government we step  forward  and provide that support and we will  continue to do so  steve you want to take a second yes  thank you dan so of course back in march  we were at the very start of this  pandemic uh we could see  uh infection rates hospitalizations  rising incredibly rapidly much more  rapidly than they are  in the autumn yeah even and of course we  could see around europe as well  health systems coming under tremendous  pressure so northern italy was a  good example uh from those high  infection rates  uh testing was more limited so we had  less information about uh  exactly where where the virus was and  and for all those reasons it was  absolutely critical  that we were able to create the capacity  uh in the nhs to deal with that wave of  infections  not knowing how large it was going to be  and in fact not knowing how the public  would respond  to the measures were being put in place  by the government  of course they responded magnificently  we know that so it was right that we  that we ensured that the nhs was ready  for that  we had the capacity of course we had the  nightingale on top  uh but of course we don't want people we  don't want to step down  elective surgery where it's all possible  and indeed that's why  in the second wave this autumn we  haven't done that  we are keeping elective care going  our clinicians have done a fantastic job  over the summer are beginning to catch  up cancer work is back where it was last  year  there is more work to do and of course  the key thing here is  as infection rates rise and hospitals  come under pressure  we've seen that in areas of the country  with high community rates  then once again uh there is a  possibility that  that elective care gets gets affected  and our staff don't want that to happen  uh our staff want to get on  treat people who are on waiting lists  and to make sure that those waiting  lists  come down and of course you know we're  all sorry that people have been affected  in this way none of us  as clinicians none of us as managers  want this to happen we want to get on  and treat people the public can help us  with this task  so it's the same message again but it's  it's absolutely true that if we  collectively as the public once again  as we did in the spring keep to the  guidance drive down infection rates then  that is the best way to ensure  that the nhs can do what it always does  which is to  treat everybody with covid people who  don't have covid  ensure that those waiting times don't  increase and that we can get on  and manage the backlog there isn't a  clinician  in the land that doesn't want to do that  we will  absolutely do our best for our patients  and the public can help us uh by doing  uh  their best too great thanks steve thanks  stan for the for the question  we're now going to sam coats at sky  samhain  hello secretary of state we've seen  turbulence  in downing street this week things seem  fluid  who should president-elect joe biden's  chief of staff call  in downing street if he has an urgent  issue to discuss  and professor price with the uk's  failure to strike a trade deal with the  eu  make any difference at all to the uk's  ability to  source coronavirus vaccine doses  sam let me let me take that uh that  question from from you um  so the first thing i would say to you is  that as you know the prime minister has  had  a a very positive call with president  elect biden  and uh he's congratulated him of course  congratulated  the vice president-elect uh who uh with  her background and also the fact  uh that she will be the first uh female  uh vice president of america of course  has achieved something really quite  historic  uh and uh the prime minister's um talked  about all the range of areas  where we have our uh we're able 